research/audit/wave5-topology-countability.provenance.jsonl:10:{"id":"lem-cellularity-is-well-defined","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"established-knowledge","urls":["https://www1.essex.ac.uk/maths/people/fremlin/chap5a.pdf"],"rationale":"ESCALATED TO ALPHA for the evidence class. Fremlin 5A4A asserts the raw supremum defining c(X) by writing it down; no located source states its well-definedness as a separate proposition, because under AC it is immediate from 'every set has a cardinality' plus 'every nonempty set of ordinals has a least element'. This library splits it out so that def-weight-density-and-character / def-lindelof-degree-and-cellularity can discharge their justified_by obligations honestly. That is a recoverable restatement of established set theory, so under the positive-determination rule it is ai-altered and NOT ai-generated; recorded as established-knowledge pending Alpha's independent concurrence. The local proof is written from lem-ordinal-trichotomy and thm-well-ordering-theorem in the library's numbered-step format, hence ai-generated.","alpha_concurred":true,"at":"2026-08-05","escalated_to_alpha":true,"alpha_rationale":"Verified against the cited source directly (pdftotext of chap5a.pdf, 5A4A): Fremlin defines w(X) as \"the least cardinal of any base for T\" (a), d(X) as \"the smallest cardinal of any dense subset\" (c), c(X) as \"sup{#(G) : G subset of T\\{empty} is disjoint}\" (d), L(X) as \"the least cardinal kappa such that every open cover of X has a subcover with cardinal at most kappa\" (g)(i), and chi(x,X) as \"the smallest cardinal of any base of neighbourhoods of x\" with chi(X) = sup_x chi(x,X) (h). These match the library definitions exactly, including the RAW (un-normalized, no \"+omega\") convention that def-weight-density-and-character and def-lindelof-degree-and-cellularity declare. Fremlin writes each minimum/supremum down without proving it exists, exactly as Beta reported: the source presupposes well-definedness in a definition rather than stating it as a proposition. A definition that presupposes a least element is NOT a semantically identical statement of the proposition that the least element exists, so `semantic-source` would overclaim and `established-knowledge` is the honest class. ALPHA CONCURS that the statement is standard established mathematics: under AC every set has a cardinality, the relevant families are sets (subfamilies of the topology, subsets of X), so the cardinalities form a nonempty set of cardinals, which has a least member and a set-sized supremum. This is routine set-theoretic topology (Juhasz, Cardinal Functions in Topology; Engelking, General Topology) and carries no positive indication of AI novelty, so the positive-determination standard forbids `ai-generated`. Statement stays `ai-altered`. SCOPE OF THIS CONCURRENCE: it certifies the STATEMENT as standard. It is not a proof certification — proof provenance stays `ai-generated` and these items remain in the A6 CRITICAL refuter set.","alpha_at":"2026-08-05"}
research/audit/wave5-topology-countability.provenance.jsonl:35:{"id":"rem-countability-axiom-implication-and-choice-ledger","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"not-applicable","evidence":"established-knowledge","urls":["https://math.ucr.edu/~res/math205C-2016/gentop-notes.pdf"],"rationale":"ESCALATED TO ALPHA for the evidence class. Orientation prose: it restates the page's own implication chain, records that the raw convention makes the metric theorem d = w rather than a blanket equality, and ledgers where countable choice is spent. Every individual assertion is proved on the page and each is standard; the assembled per-page ledger has no counterpart in a source, which is why no URL carries it. Recoverable restatement, so ai-altered and not ai-generated; established-knowledge pending Alpha concurrence. Remark, no proof component. I verified each claim in the remark against the items it summarises.","alpha_concurred":true,"at":"2026-08-05","escalated_to_alpha":true,"alpha_rationale":"ALPHA CONCURS. Read the remark on disk with the standing remark-suspicion rule and checked each component assertion independently. Second countable => first countable (no choice); with AC_omega, second countable => separable and Lindelof (both standard, both genuinely need countable choice); separable => ccc (standard, and choice-free since a countable dense set is indexed). The raw-convention claim is correct and is the sharp point of the remark: with un-normalized cardinal functions the metric result is d = w (confirmed against Fremlin 5A4B(h)(i), which states d(X) = w(X) for metrizable X under those same raw conventions) and a blanket equality of all five functions is FALSE — a finite discrete space on n > 1 points has w = d = c = n but chi = 1, so the remark is right to refuse the blanket form. Every component is standard; only the per-page assembly is local, and assembling proved standard facts into orientation prose is not AI-invented mathematics. `ai-altered` / `established-knowledge` upheld.","alpha_at":"2026-08-05"}
research/audit/wave5-topology-countability.provenance.jsonl:45:{"id":"rem-euclidean-topology-dictionary","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"not-applicable","evidence":"established-knowledge","urls":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Product_topology","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Norm_(mathematics)"],"rationale":"ESCALATED TO ALPHA for the evidence class. Orientation prose fixing the page's conventions: the product, d_1/d_2/d_infinity metric, and arbitrary-norm topologies on R^n all coincide, and for n = 1 they agree with the real-line topology. Each component claim is standard and is proved in this library (lem-product-topology-on-rn claim 3; thm-all-norms-on-rn-are-equivalent; lem-real-and-metric-notions-agree), and I verified the citations against those items. The assembled per-page dictionary has no source counterpart, hence established-knowledge rather than a URL claim. Remark, no proof component. SEPARATE DEFECT: its recorded reference is 'J. R. Munkres, Topology, 2nd ed., SS19, 20' pointing at the Wikipedia article about the PERSON - see finding N1.","alpha_concurred":true,"at":"2026-08-05","escalated_to_alpha":true,"alpha_rationale":"ALPHA CONCURS. Checked each component assertion: the product topology on R^n coincides with the metric topologies of d_1, d_2 and d_infinity; every norm on R^n is equivalent to the Euclidean norm and so induces that same topology; for n = 1 the Euclidean metric is |s - t| and the metric and real-line formulations of continuity and compactness agree. All three are standard and each is proved in-library by the cited deps. Only the per-page dictionary assembly is local, which is not novelty. `ai-altered` / `established-knowledge` upheld. NOTE, NOT part of this concurrence: Beta finding N1 stands — this item's first reference titles \"J. R. Munkres, Topology, 2nd ed., SS19, 20\" but its URL is https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/James_Munkres, the biography of the person, not the cited topology text. That is a citation-precision defect for A6 repair. It does not affect this evidence class, which waives the URL requirement outright, and the remaining two URLs do support the content.","alpha_at":"2026-08-05"}
research/audit/wave5-real-analysis.provenance.jsonl:30:{"id":"cex-unbounded-with-convergent-subsequence","statement":"ai-generated","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"trivial","urls":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bolzano%E2%80%93Weierstrass_theorem","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Subsequence"],"rationale":"Positive novelty determination, on the witness. That the converse of Bolzano-Weierstrass fails is established (and is tagged as such on the false statement this item refutes, fs-convergent-subsequence-implies-bounded), but the WITNESS is bespoke: the interleaving 1, 1, 2, 1, 3, 1, 4, ... defined by cases on the even/odd partition of N supplied by lem-alternating-sequence, together with the computation that its subsequential limit set is exactly {1}. No source selects this construction; the item builds it from this page's own machinery. That is the bespoke checkable-witness species the AUDIT-WORKFLOW sect. 6 trivial row assigns to ai-generated with generation.role: counterexample, on the wave-0 precedent Alpha upheld. Truth verified directly from the stated dependencies: the definition is total and single-valued because e and o partition N and each is injective; the subsequence along o is constantly 1, hence convergent; for any real M the Archimedean property gives m >= 1 with |M| < m, and y_{e_{m-1}} = m > |M| >= M, so no M bounds |y_n|; and any convergent subsequence meets the range of e only finitely often (its values j+1 exceed every bound), so it is eventually constantly 1 and its limit is 1, giving SL = {1}. Blast radius nil: zero deps consumers corpus-wide. Recorded for Alpha as a borderline - ai-altered under established-knowledge is defensible if Alpha judges the interleaving itself to be canonical.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-05"}
research/audit/wave2-combinatorics.provenance.jsonl:8:{"id": "def-intersecting-family", "statement": "ai-altered", "proof": "not-applicable", "evidence": "semantic-source", "urls": ["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Erd%C5%91s%E2%80%93Ko%E2%80%93Rado_theorem"], "rationale": "Wikipedia, Erdős-Ko-Rado theorem, Statement and 'Maximum and maximal intersecting families': a family of distinct r-element subsets of an n-element set in which 'each two subsets share at least one element', and the star family formed by fixing an element x and taking all r-sets containing it. Alpha fetched the page at HTTP 200 on 2026-08-03 and read that clause. Semantically identical to the local definition, which fixes the uniform level [A]^k and names the star S_a. Alpha (claude-opus-5) independently determines the statement to be standard established mathematics; the A4 established-knowledge waiver is withdrawn because a working source was located and fetched, the earlier failure being that the Beta sandbox could not resolve external DNS.", "alpha_concurred": true, "at": "2026-08-03"}
research/audit/wave2-combinatorics.provenance.jsonl:9:{"id": "def-sunflower", "statement": "ai-altered", "proof": "not-applicable", "evidence": "semantic-source", "urls": ["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sunflower_(mathematics)"], "rationale": "Wikipedia, Sunflower (mathematics), 'Formal definition': 'The collection W is a sunflower (or Delta-system) if there is a subset S of U such that for each distinct A and B in W, we have A intersect B = S.' Alpha fetched the page at HTTP 200 on 2026-08-03 and read that clause. Semantically identical; the local form additionally names r >= 2, the core, the petals, and k-uniformity, and states the equivalent 'all pairwise intersections equal' formulation the same article gives. Alpha (claude-opus-5) independently determines the statement to be standard established mathematics; the A4 established-knowledge waiver is withdrawn because a working source was located and fetched, the earlier failure being that the Beta sandbox could not resolve external DNS.", "alpha_concurred": true, "at": "2026-08-03"}
research/audit/wave2-combinatorics.provenance.jsonl:23:{"id": "lem-katona-cyclic-interval-bound", "statement": "ai-altered", "proof": "ai-altered", "evidence": "semantic-source", "urls": ["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Erd%C5%91s%E2%80%93Ko%E2%80%93Rado_theorem"], "rationale": "Wikipedia, Erdős-Ko-Rado theorem, 'Proof' (Katona 1972): 'Katona's key observation is that at most r intervals from a single cyclic order may belong to A.' Alpha fetched the page at HTTP 200 on 2026-08-03 and read that clause. Semantically identical with the article's r renamed k; the local statement carries the same n >= 2k hypothesis the article records as necessary. The proof follows the standard cyclic-interval argument in locally stratified form. Alpha (claude-opus-5) independently determines the statement to be standard established mathematics; the A4 established-knowledge waiver is withdrawn because a working source was located and fetched, the earlier failure being that the Beta sandbox could not resolve external DNS.", "alpha_concurred": true, "at": "2026-08-03"}
research/audit/wave2-combinatorics.provenance.jsonl:25:{"id": "lem-sunflower-maximal-disjoint-subfamily", "statement": "ai-altered", "proof": "ai-altered", "evidence": "semantic-source", "urls": ["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sunflower_(mathematics)"], "rationale": "Wikipedia, Sunflower (mathematics), 'Sunflower lemma' proof: 'consider A_1, ..., A_t in W to be a maximal collection of pairwise disjoint sets (that is, A_i intersect A_j is the empty set unless i = j, and every set in W intersects with some A_i). Because we assumed that W had no sunflower of size r, and a collection of pairwise disjoint sets is a sunflower, t < r' and 'the cardinality of A is bounded by kt <= k(r-1)'. Alpha fetched the page at HTTP 200 on 2026-08-03 and read those clauses. That is exactly this lemma, stated here as a named decomposition step rather than inline. Alpha (claude-opus-5) independently determines the statement to be standard established mathematics; the A4 established-knowledge waiver is withdrawn because a working source was located and fetched, the earlier failure being that the Beta sandbox could not resolve external DNS.", "alpha_concurred": true, "at": "2026-08-03"}
research/audit/wave2-combinatorics.provenance.jsonl:26:{"id": "thm-erdos-rado-sunflower-lemma", "statement": "ai-altered", "proof": "ai-altered", "evidence": "semantic-source", "urls": ["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sunflower_(mathematics)"], "rationale": "Wikipedia, Sunflower (mathematics), 'Sunflower lemma' (Erdős & Rado 1960, p. 86): 'if k and r are positive integers, then a set system W of cardinality greater than k!(r-1)^k of sets of cardinality k contains a sunflower with at least r sets.' Alpha fetched the page at HTTP 200 on 2026-08-03 and read that clause. The local statement is the same bound with k >= 0 admitted (vacuous at k = 0, since only one 0-element set exists). Proof follows the standard induction in locally stratified form. Alpha (claude-opus-5) independently determines the statement to be standard established mathematics; the A4 established-knowledge waiver is withdrawn because a working source was located and fetched, the earlier failure being that the Beta sandbox could not resolve external DNS.", "alpha_concurred": true, "at": "2026-08-03"}
research/audit/wave2-combinatorics.provenance.jsonl:32:{"id": "fs-erdos-ko-rado-without-the-ground-set-bound", "statement": "ai-altered", "proof": "ai-generated", "evidence": "established-knowledge", "urls": ["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Erd%C5%91s%E2%80%93Ko%E2%80%93Rado_theorem"], "rationale": "CORRECTION (Alpha, A6, upholding an independent Sol certifier's refusal): semantic-source overstated the match. The Erdos-Ko-Rado article states the theorem WITH the hypothesis n >= 2r and explains that the hypothesis cannot be dropped ('The requirement that n >= 2r is necessary for the problem to be nontrivial: when n < 2r, all r-element sets intersect, and the largest intersecting family consists of all r-element sets, with binom(n, r)'); it does not state this item's false claim, which is that theorem with a load-bearing hypothesis deleted, so the two are not semantically identical. Reclassified to established-knowledge: 'Erdos-Ko-Rado without its ground-set bound' is a standard, universally recognised false statement of extremal set theory, and Alpha (claude-opus-5) independently concurs it is standard. The URL is retained in sources.references because it is the exact true theorem this item perturbs and it records why the hypothesis is needed. The finite refutation is locally composed and directly checked.", "alpha_concurred": true, "at": "2026-08-03"}
research/audit/wave2-combinatorics.provenance.jsonl:37:{"id": "ex-an-erdos-ko-rado-star", "statement": "ai-altered", "proof": "ai-generated", "evidence": "semantic-source", "urls": ["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Erd%C5%91s%E2%80%93Ko%E2%80%93Rado_theorem"], "rationale": "Wikipedia, Erdős-Ko-Rado theorem, 'Maximum and maximal intersecting families': 'A simple way to construct an intersecting family of r-element sets whose size exactly matches the Erdős-Ko-Rado bound is to choose any fixed element x ... The number of sets is binom(n-1, r-1), because after the fixed element is chosen there remain n-1 other elements to choose, and each set chooses r-1 of them.' Alpha fetched the page at HTTP 200 on 2026-08-03 and read that clause. Semantically identical to the local star construction and its cardinality. The finite verification is locally composed and directly checked. Alpha (claude-opus-5) independently determines the statement to be standard established mathematics; the A4 established-knowledge waiver is withdrawn because a working source was located and fetched, the earlier failure being that the Beta sandbox could not resolve external DNS.", "alpha_concurred": true, "at": "2026-08-03"}
research/audit/wave2-combinatorics.provenance.jsonl:39:{"id": "cex-erdos-ko-rado-fails-below-two-k", "statement": "ai-altered", "proof": "ai-generated", "evidence": "semantic-source", "urls": ["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Erd%C5%91s%E2%80%93Ko%E2%80%93Rado_theorem"], "rationale": "Wikipedia, Erdős-Ko-Rado theorem, 'Statement': 'when n < 2r, all r-element sets intersect, and the largest intersecting family consists of all r-element sets, with binom(n, r).' Alpha fetched the page at HTTP 200 on 2026-08-03 and read that clause. That is exactly the witness recorded here for k < n < 2k. The finite verification is locally composed and directly checked. Alpha (claude-opus-5) independently determines the statement to be standard established mathematics; the A4 established-knowledge waiver is withdrawn because a working source was located and fetched, the earlier failure being that the Beta sandbox could not resolve external DNS.", "alpha_concurred": true, "at": "2026-08-03"}
research/audit/wave1-real-analysis.provenance.jsonl:22:{"id":"lem-of-square-monotone","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"established-knowledge","urls":[],"rationale":"The three equivalences are standard ordered-field consequences of b^2-a^2=(b-a)(b+a) and |x|^2=x^2, but no reputable source with the complete compound statement surfaced after targeted searches. The formulation is recognizable established mathematics rather than an invented claim; Alpha independently concurred from the current statement and local derivation at A6.","alpha_concurred":true,"at":"2026-08-03"}
