research/audit/wave5-topology-countability.provenance.jsonl:35:{"id":"rem-countability-axiom-implication-and-choice-ledger","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"not-applicable","evidence":"established-knowledge","urls":["https://math.ucr.edu/~res/math205C-2016/gentop-notes.pdf"],"rationale":"ESCALATED TO ALPHA for the evidence class. Orientation prose: it restates the page's own implication chain, records that the raw convention makes the metric theorem d = w rather than a blanket equality, and ledgers where countable choice is spent. Every individual assertion is proved on the page and each is standard; the assembled per-page ledger has no counterpart in a source, which is why no URL carries it. Recoverable restatement, so ai-altered and not ai-generated; established-knowledge pending Alpha concurrence. Remark, no proof component. I verified each claim in the remark against the items it summarises.","alpha_concurred":true,"at":"2026-08-05","escalated_to_alpha":true,"alpha_rationale":"ALPHA CONCURS. Read the remark on disk with the standing remark-suspicion rule and checked each component assertion independently. Second countable => first countable (no choice); with AC_omega, second countable => separable and Lindelof (both standard, both genuinely need countable choice); separable => ccc (standard, and choice-free since a countable dense set is indexed). The raw-convention claim is correct and is the sharp point of the remark: with un-normalized cardinal functions the metric result is d = w (confirmed against Fremlin 5A4B(h)(i), which states d(X) = w(X) for metrizable X under those same raw conventions) and a blanket equality of all five functions is FALSE — a finite discrete space on n > 1 points has w = d = c = n but chi = 1, so the remark is right to refuse the blanket form. Every component is standard; only the per-page assembly is local, and assembling proved standard facts into orientation prose is not AI-invented mathematics. `ai-altered` / `established-knowledge` upheld.","alpha_at":"2026-08-05"}
research/audit/wave5-topology-countability.provenance.jsonl:45:{"id":"rem-euclidean-topology-dictionary","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"not-applicable","evidence":"established-knowledge","urls":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Product_topology","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Norm_(mathematics)"],"rationale":"ESCALATED TO ALPHA for the evidence class. Orientation prose fixing the page's conventions: the product, d_1/d_2/d_infinity metric, and arbitrary-norm topologies on R^n all coincide, and for n = 1 they agree with the real-line topology. Each component claim is standard and is proved in this library (lem-product-topology-on-rn claim 3; thm-all-norms-on-rn-are-equivalent; lem-real-and-metric-notions-agree), and I verified the citations against those items. The assembled per-page dictionary has no source counterpart, hence established-knowledge rather than a URL claim. Remark, no proof component. SEPARATE DEFECT: its recorded reference is 'J. R. Munkres, Topology, 2nd ed., SS19, 20' pointing at the Wikipedia article about the PERSON - see finding N1.","alpha_concurred":true,"at":"2026-08-05","escalated_to_alpha":true,"alpha_rationale":"ALPHA CONCURS. Checked each component assertion: the product topology on R^n coincides with the metric topologies of d_1, d_2 and d_infinity; every norm on R^n is equivalent to the Euclidean norm and so induces that same topology; for n = 1 the Euclidean metric is |s - t| and the metric and real-line formulations of continuity and compactness agree. All three are standard and each is proved in-library by the cited deps. Only the per-page dictionary assembly is local, which is not novelty. `ai-altered` / `established-knowledge` upheld. NOTE, NOT part of this concurrence: Beta finding N1 stands — this item's first reference titles \"J. R. Munkres, Topology, 2nd ed., SS19, 20\" but its URL is https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/James_Munkres, the biography of the person, not the cited topology text. That is a citation-precision defect for A6 repair. It does not affect this evidence class, which waives the URL requirement outright, and the remaining two URLs do support the content.","alpha_at":"2026-08-05"}
research/audit/wave0-number-theory.provenance.jsonl:32:{"id":"ex-euclidean-algorithm-on-consecutive-fibonacci-numbers","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"established-knowledge","urls":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Euclidean_algorithm","https://mathworld.wolfram.com/EuclideanAlgorithm.html","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fibonacci_sequence"],"rationale":"Two established results, neither located in the exact form the item states. (1) Consecutive Fibonacci numbers are coprime: standard, and a special case of gcd(F_m,F_n) = F_gcd(m,n); no clean HTTP-200 statement surfaced (ProofWiki returns 403 to the fetcher). (2) The division count: Wikipedia and MathWorld both record that consecutive Fibonacci numbers are the worst case for the Euclidean algorithm (Lame 1844), and the standard form of that analysis is that (F_{n+2}, F_{n+1}) needs exactly n divisions, which is the item claim with n = m+1; the quotient pattern (1 repeated, then a final 2) is the routine content of the same analysis. Both are recoverable restatements of established mathematics, not invented claims, so the tag is ai-altered under evidence established-knowledge and REQUIRES Alpha concurrence. The indexing convention F_0 = 0 is stated by the item and matches the Wikipedia zeroth-element convention. Proof is a locally written double induction.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-02"}
research/audit/wave0-number-theory.provenance.jsonl:36:{"id":"ex-euclidean-algorithm-on-consecutive-fibonacci-numbers","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"established-knowledge","urls":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Euclidean_algorithm","https://mathworld.wolfram.com/EuclideanAlgorithm.html","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fibonacci_sequence"],"rationale":"Two established results, neither located in the exact form the item states. (1) Consecutive Fibonacci numbers are coprime: standard, and a special case of gcd(F_m,F_n) = F_gcd(m,n); no clean HTTP-200 statement surfaced (ProofWiki returns 403 to the fetcher). (2) The division count: Wikipedia and MathWorld both record that consecutive Fibonacci numbers are the worst case for the Euclidean algorithm (Lame 1844), and the standard form of that analysis is that (F_{n+2}, F_{n+1}) needs exactly n divisions, which is the item claim with n = m+1; the quotient pattern (1 repeated, then a final 2) is the routine content of the same analysis. Both are recoverable restatements of established mathematics, not invented claims, so the tag is ai-altered under evidence established-knowledge and REQUIRES Alpha concurrence. The indexing convention F_0 = 0 is stated by the item and matches the Wikipedia zeroth-element convention. Proof is a locally written double induction.\n\nALPHA CONCURS (2026-08-02). Both components are standard established mathematics. (1) gcd(F_{k+1}, F_k) = 1 for all k is a textbook result and the k-th case of gcd(F_m,F_n) = F_{gcd(m,n)}. (2) The division count is the standard statement of the Euclidean algorithm's Fibonacci worst case (Lame 1844): on (F_{n+2}, F_{n+1}) with n >= 1 the algorithm performs exactly n divisions, which is the item's claim at n = m+1; the quotient pattern (1 repeated, then a terminal 2 at (F_3,F_2) = (2,1)) is the routine content of the same analysis, not an invented embellishment. Alpha verified the claim numerically at m = 0,1,2: (2,1) -> 1 division q=2; (3,2) -> 2 divisions q=1,2; (5,3) -> 3 divisions q=1,1,2. The F_0 = 0 indexing is stated by the item and matches the standard convention, and the item explicitly disclaims Lame's optimality statement. ai-altered under the established-knowledge waiver; not ai-generated, since nothing here is AI-invented.","alpha_concurred":true,"at":"2026-08-02","alpha_reviewer":"audit-alpha (claude-opus-5)"}
research/audit/wave0-number-theory.provenance.jsonl:39:{"id":"lem-coprime-divides-product","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Euclid%27s_lemma","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coprime_integers"],"rationale":"Clause 1 is the sourced generalisation of Euclid lemma: \"If an integer n divides the product ab of two integers, and is coprime with a, then n divides b\". Clause 2 (a | c, b | c, gcd(a,b) = 1 imply ab | c) is standard but was not located verbatim on either page; it is proved here from clause 1, so the item is a recoverable restatement rather than an invented claim. Proof adapts the sourced Bezout argument.\n\nALPHA CLASSIFICATION REVIEW E3 (2026-08-02): UPHELD ai-altered / semantic-source. Clause 1 is the sourced generalisation of Euclid's lemma, located verbatim. Clause 2 (a | c, b | c, gcd(a,b) = 1 implies ab | c) is standard textbook number theory, and decisively it is PROVED IN THIS ITEM from clause 1 in two steps, so the merged statement introduces no unsourced mathematical content. The semantic-source evidence class explicitly covers a statement 'reformulated, renotated, specialised/merged without changing content', which is exactly this shape. Keeping semantic-source also keeps a reader-visible URL on the item, which is strictly better than moving to the established-knowledge waiver. No change.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-02","alpha_reviewer":"audit-alpha (claude-opus-5)"}
research/audit/wave0-real-analysis-cauchy.provenance.jsonl:15:{"id":"lem-rat-inverse-well-defined","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"established-knowledge","urls":[],"rationale":"ESCALATION-LEVEL NOTE for Alpha: well-definedness of the reciprocal [(a,b)]^-1 := [(b,a)] on Q \\ {0} is a standard step in every set-theoretic construction of Q (Landau, Tao Analysis I S4.2, Enderton Ch. 5), but no open source stating it as a named lemma surfaced. It is not an invented claim: it is the routine representative-independence obligation attached to the sourced reciprocal formula of Wikipedia Rational number, and is directly verifiable from def-rationals in one line (ab' = a'b <=> ba' = b'a by commutativity of Z). Recorded ai-altered/established-knowledge pending Alpha's independent concurrence. Local proof written here.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-02"}
