research/audit/wave2-combinatorics.provenance.jsonl:23:{"id": "lem-katona-cyclic-interval-bound", "statement": "ai-altered", "proof": "ai-altered", "evidence": "semantic-source", "urls": ["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Erd%C5%91s%E2%80%93Ko%E2%80%93Rado_theorem"], "rationale": "Wikipedia, Erdős-Ko-Rado theorem, 'Proof' (Katona 1972): 'Katona's key observation is that at most r intervals from a single cyclic order may belong to A.' Alpha fetched the page at HTTP 200 on 2026-08-03 and read that clause. Semantically identical with the article's r renamed k; the local statement carries the same n >= 2k hypothesis the article records as necessary. The proof follows the standard cyclic-interval argument in locally stratified form. Alpha (claude-opus-5) independently determines the statement to be standard established mathematics; the A4 established-knowledge waiver is withdrawn because a working source was located and fetched, the earlier failure being that the Beta sandbox could not resolve external DNS.", "alpha_concurred": true, "at": "2026-08-03"}
research/audit/wave2-combinatorics.provenance.jsonl:25:{"id": "lem-sunflower-maximal-disjoint-subfamily", "statement": "ai-altered", "proof": "ai-altered", "evidence": "semantic-source", "urls": ["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sunflower_(mathematics)"], "rationale": "Wikipedia, Sunflower (mathematics), 'Sunflower lemma' proof: 'consider A_1, ..., A_t in W to be a maximal collection of pairwise disjoint sets (that is, A_i intersect A_j is the empty set unless i = j, and every set in W intersects with some A_i). Because we assumed that W had no sunflower of size r, and a collection of pairwise disjoint sets is a sunflower, t < r' and 'the cardinality of A is bounded by kt <= k(r-1)'. Alpha fetched the page at HTTP 200 on 2026-08-03 and read those clauses. That is exactly this lemma, stated here as a named decomposition step rather than inline. Alpha (claude-opus-5) independently determines the statement to be standard established mathematics; the A4 established-knowledge waiver is withdrawn because a working source was located and fetched, the earlier failure being that the Beta sandbox could not resolve external DNS.", "alpha_concurred": true, "at": "2026-08-03"}
research/audit/wave2-combinatorics.provenance.jsonl:26:{"id": "thm-erdos-rado-sunflower-lemma", "statement": "ai-altered", "proof": "ai-altered", "evidence": "semantic-source", "urls": ["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sunflower_(mathematics)"], "rationale": "Wikipedia, Sunflower (mathematics), 'Sunflower lemma' (Erdős & Rado 1960, p. 86): 'if k and r are positive integers, then a set system W of cardinality greater than k!(r-1)^k of sets of cardinality k contains a sunflower with at least r sets.' Alpha fetched the page at HTTP 200 on 2026-08-03 and read that clause. The local statement is the same bound with k >= 0 admitted (vacuous at k = 0, since only one 0-element set exists). Proof follows the standard induction in locally stratified form. Alpha (claude-opus-5) independently determines the statement to be standard established mathematics; the A4 established-knowledge waiver is withdrawn because a working source was located and fetched, the earlier failure being that the Beta sandbox could not resolve external DNS.", "alpha_concurred": true, "at": "2026-08-03"}
research/audit/wave2-combinatorics.provenance.jsonl:32:{"id": "fs-erdos-ko-rado-without-the-ground-set-bound", "statement": "ai-altered", "proof": "ai-generated", "evidence": "established-knowledge", "urls": ["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Erd%C5%91s%E2%80%93Ko%E2%80%93Rado_theorem"], "rationale": "CORRECTION (Alpha, A6, upholding an independent Sol certifier's refusal): semantic-source overstated the match. The Erdos-Ko-Rado article states the theorem WITH the hypothesis n >= 2r and explains that the hypothesis cannot be dropped ('The requirement that n >= 2r is necessary for the problem to be nontrivial: when n < 2r, all r-element sets intersect, and the largest intersecting family consists of all r-element sets, with binom(n, r)'); it does not state this item's false claim, which is that theorem with a load-bearing hypothesis deleted, so the two are not semantically identical. Reclassified to established-knowledge: 'Erdos-Ko-Rado without its ground-set bound' is a standard, universally recognised false statement of extremal set theory, and Alpha (claude-opus-5) independently concurs it is standard. The URL is retained in sources.references because it is the exact true theorem this item perturbs and it records why the hypothesis is needed. The finite refutation is locally composed and directly checked.", "alpha_concurred": true, "at": "2026-08-03"}
