items/cex-separately-continuous-not-jointly-continuous.md:100:- **Nothing is claimed about $g$ away from the origin.** The refutation needs only the behaviour of $g$ at $0$ together with the two partial functions, and that is all that is proved. In particular this item does **not** assert that $g$ is continuous at the points $p \ne 0$, true though that is; establishing it would need an algebra of continuous real-valued functions on a metric domain, which [[thm-componentwise-limits-and-continuity]] provides for sums, scalar multiples and inner products but not for quotients.
