items/lem-t0-t1-and-hausdorff-are-hereditary.md:2:id: lem-t0-t1-and-hausdorff-are-hereditary
items/lem-t0-t1-and-hausdorff-are-hereditary.md:4:title: "$T_0$, $T_1$, and Hausdorffness are hereditary"
items/lem-t0-t1-and-hausdorff-are-hereditary.md:26:## Statement
items/lem-t0-t1-and-hausdorff-are-hereditary.md:28:The properties $T_0$, $T_1$, and Hausdorffness are hereditary in the sense of [[def-hereditary-property]].
items/lem-t0-t1-and-hausdorff-are-hereditary.md:36:[F2] $T_0$ distinguishes a distinct pair by one open set, $T_1$ separates each point from the other by an open set, and Hausdorffness separates a distinct pair by disjoint open sets ([[def-t0-and-t1-spaces]], [[def-hausdorff-space]]).
items/lem-t0-t1-and-hausdorff-are-hereditary.md:44:1.2 If $X$ is $T_1$, apply the preceding trace argument separately to the two open sets supplied by the $T_1$ condition, so each of $x,y$ has an open neighbourhood in $S$ missing the other. [F1, F2]
items/lem-t0-t1-and-hausdorff-are-hereditary.md:46:1.3 If $X$ is Hausdorff, choose disjoint open $U,V\subseteq X$ containing $x,y$ respectively; $S\cap U$ and $S\cap V$ are disjoint open neighbourhoods in $S$. [F1, F2]
items/lem-products-preserve-t0-t1-and-hausdorff.md:2:id: lem-products-preserve-t0-t1-and-hausdorff
items/lem-products-preserve-t0-t1-and-hausdorff.md:4:title: "Arbitrary products preserve $T_0$, $T_1$, and Hausdorffness"
items/lem-products-preserve-t0-t1-and-hausdorff.md:26:## Statement
items/lem-products-preserve-t0-t1-and-hausdorff.md:28:For any family $(X_i)_{i\in I}$, if every $X_i$ is $T_0$, respectively $T_1$, respectively Hausdorff, then $\prod_{i\in I}X_i$ is respectively $T_0$, $T_1$, respectively Hausdorff. The empty product is included.
items/lem-products-preserve-t0-t1-and-hausdorff.md:36:[F2] The $T_0$, $T_1$, and Hausdorff conditions are respectively the stated one-sided, two-sided, and disjoint-open separations of distinct points ([[def-t0-and-t1-spaces]], [[def-hausdorff-space]]).
items/lem-products-preserve-t0-t1-and-hausdorff.md:46:1.3 For a $T_1$ factor, pull back the two open sets separating $x_i$ from $y_i$ and $y_i$ from $x_i$. [F1, F2]
items/lem-products-preserve-t0-t1-and-hausdorff.md:48:1.4 For a Hausdorff factor, pull back disjoint open neighbourhoods of $x_i,y_i$; their inverse images remain disjoint. [F1, F2]
