research/audit/wave6-real-analysis-limits.provenance.jsonl:16:{"id":"lem-nth-root-of-n-tends-to-one","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"established-knowledge","urls":[],"rationale":"ESCALATED TO ALPHA. The limit n^(1/n)->1 is standard and the displayed bound 1<=n^(1/n)<=1+2/sqrt(n) is a routine weakened binomial estimate, but no reputable source with this exact two-clause bound and zero-based reindexing surfaced. It is recoverable established mathematics; the explicit inequality proof is local.","alpha_concurred":true,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave6-real-analysis-limits.provenance.jsonl:19:{"id":"lem-power-over-geometric-null","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"established-knowledge","urls":[],"rationale":"ESCALATED TO ALPHA. Polynomial growth is dominated by every geometric sequence of ratio greater than one; the local positive-rational exponent and base 1+p formulation is a standard specialization. No located reputable source stated this exact domain and quantifier package, so it is recorded as established knowledge pending independent concurrence, not as an invented claim.","alpha_concurred":true,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave6-real-analysis-limits.provenance.jsonl:20:{"id":"lem-factorial-beats-geometric","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"established-knowledge","urls":[],"rationale":"ESCALATED TO ALPHA. The limit x^k/k! -> 0 for every real x is the standard factorial-versus-exponential estimate used in the exponential series. No located reputable source stated precisely this all-real sequence claim, so the statement is established knowledge pending concurrence; the tail squeeze proof is local.","alpha_concurred":true,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave6-real-analysis-limits.provenance.jsonl:22:{"id":"fs-limsup-additive","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"established-knowledge","urls":[],"rationale":"ESCALATED TO ALPHA. This is the standard false strengthening obtained by replacing the sourced limsup subadditivity inequality by equality; the alternating counterexample is canonical. The true theorem and counterexample were located, but no source stated this exact false universal as a proposition, so concurrence is required.","alpha_concurred":true,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave6-real-analysis-limits.provenance.jsonl:25:{"id":"ex-subsequential-limits-fill-unit-interval","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"established-knowledge","urls":[],"rationale":"ESCALATED TO ALPHA. Enumerating increasingly fine rational grids in [0,1] so that the finite subsequential-limit set is exactly [0,1] is a standard textbook construction. Searches found the equivalent enumeration-of-rationals construction but not this exact triangular block sequence; it is recoverable established mathematics rather than positively novel.","alpha_concurred":true,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave6-real-analysis-limits.provenance.jsonl:26:{"id":"ex-ratio-fails-root-succeeds","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"established-knowledge","urls":[],"rationale":"ESCALATED TO ALPHA. Periodically perturbing a geometric sequence so its roots converge while consecutive ratios oscillate is a standard counterexample pattern, and UCI gives closely related alternating-base examples, but no semantically identical source for the exact exponent -k+(-1)^k surfaced. The explicit computation is local.","alpha_concurred":true,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave6-real-analysis-limits.provenance.jsonl:58:{"id":"ex-closed-interval-is-perfect","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"established-knowledge","urls":[],"rationale":"ESCALATED TO ALPHA. Every nondegenerate closed interval has no isolated points and is closed, hence is perfect; this is a canonical textbook example assembled from the standard definitions. No located source stated this exact worked example and its second uncountability conclusion, so established-knowledge concurrence is requested.","alpha_concurred":true,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave6-real-analysis-limits.provenance.jsonl:62:{"id":"cex-closed-bounded-in-q-not-compact","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"established-knowledge","urls":[],"rationale":"ESCALATED TO ALPHA. The rational lower cut {q>=0:q^2<2} is the canonical example of a closed bounded subset of Q lacking compactness because a sequence approaches sqrt(2) outside Q. A reputable source with the same construction pattern but different cut was found, not this exact set; the claim is established, not novel.","alpha_concurred":true,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave6-real-analysis-limits.provenance.jsonl:63:{"id":"cex-rationals-in-an-interval-are-disconnected","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"established-knowledge","urls":[],"rationale":"ESCALATED TO ALPHA. Separating Q intersect [0,2] at a missing irrational point is the standard proof that rational intervals are disconnected. The connected-subsets-of-R theorem supplies the underlying principle, but no source for this exact witness surfaced; the explicit separation is locally verified.","alpha_concurred":true,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave6-real-analysis-limits.provenance.jsonl:65:{"id":"cex-unbounded-closed-set-not-compact","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"established-knowledge","urls":[],"rationale":"ESCALATED TO ALPHA. Z as a closed unbounded noncompact set and (0,1) as a bounded nonclosed noncompact set are canonical examples showing both Heine-Borel hypotheses are necessary. No single located source stated this exact paired item, so it is recorded as established knowledge pending concurrence.","alpha_concurred":true,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave5-topology-separation.provenance.jsonl:68:{"id":"ex-free-ultrafilter-converging-in-a-convergent-sequence-space","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"established-knowledge","urls":[],"rationale":"The construction is textbook folklore rather than a quotable statement: in the one-point compactification of the discrete natural numbers, which is the convergent-sequence space used here, any free ultrafilter on N pushed forward along the inclusion converges to the point at infinity. I searched for an exact source for this specific example and located the ingredients (free ultrafilters on N under the ultrafilter lemma, filter convergence, the space N with a point at infinity) but no single source stating the example, so it is recorded as a recoverable restatement of established material rather than as a novel claim. ESCALATED TO ALPHA for the concurrence that this evidence class requires; until that concurrence the row is not to be used as an exact- or semantic-source determination.","alpha_concurred":true,"escalated_to_alpha":true,"at":"2026-08-05","alpha_rationale":"ALPHA CONCURS. Independently checked the space and the claim from disk, and searched for a source. First, the space is what Beta says it is: the item gives infinity the neighbourhood base U_N = {infinity} u {n : n >= N}, i.e. TAILS rather than complements-of-finite-sets, so the identification with the one-point compactification of discrete N needs checking rather than assuming — and it holds, because a subset of N contains a tail iff it is cofinite (if N\\S is finite, take N = max(N\\S)+1). The two neighbourhood filters at infinity therefore coincide and the space IS the one-point compactification of discrete N, equivalently the convergent-sequence space. Second, the claim is standard on two independent routes: directly, a free ultrafilter contains no finite set, hence contains every cofinite set, hence every tail, hence converges to infinity; and structurally, the one-point compactification is compact, so every ultrafilter on it converges, while a free one cannot converge to an isolated natural. Both are textbook. Third, on the source: a search located every ingredient (compactness iff every ultrafilter converges; free ultrafilters on N as the points of beta-N minus N; the one-point compactification of a discrete space) but no single source stating THIS example as a proposition — Encyclopedia of Mathematics \"Ultrafilter\" and Norwood, \"Filter convergence and Tychonoff's theorem\" (Cornell) carry the ingredients. That is exactly the situation D2 reserves for this class, and it is why the row correctly carries no URL and does not claim the item's Wikipedia \"Ultrafilter (set theory)\" reference as a statement source: that article supports an ingredient, not the example. Folklore that every competent reader reconstructs in two lines carries no positive indication of AI novelty, so the positive-determination standard forbids `ai-generated`. Statement stays `ai-altered`. Checked separately for a title-overclaims-proof defect and found none: the title and Statement assert the existential (\"a free ultrafilter ... converges\"), and the Verification proves the existential by extending the tail filter. SCOPE: this certifies the STATEMENT as standard. Proof provenance stays `ai-generated` and the item remains subject to ordinary A6 refuter reading.","alpha_at":"2026-08-05"}
research/audit/wave0-number-theory.provenance.jsonl:32:{"id":"ex-euclidean-algorithm-on-consecutive-fibonacci-numbers","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"established-knowledge","urls":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Euclidean_algorithm","https://mathworld.wolfram.com/EuclideanAlgorithm.html","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fibonacci_sequence"],"rationale":"Two established results, neither located in the exact form the item states. (1) Consecutive Fibonacci numbers are coprime: standard, and a special case of gcd(F_m,F_n) = F_gcd(m,n); no clean HTTP-200 statement surfaced (ProofWiki returns 403 to the fetcher). (2) The division count: Wikipedia and MathWorld both record that consecutive Fibonacci numbers are the worst case for the Euclidean algorithm (Lame 1844), and the standard form of that analysis is that (F_{n+2}, F_{n+1}) needs exactly n divisions, which is the item claim with n = m+1; the quotient pattern (1 repeated, then a final 2) is the routine content of the same analysis. Both are recoverable restatements of established mathematics, not invented claims, so the tag is ai-altered under evidence established-knowledge and REQUIRES Alpha concurrence. The indexing convention F_0 = 0 is stated by the item and matches the Wikipedia zeroth-element convention. Proof is a locally written double induction.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-02"}
research/audit/wave0-number-theory.provenance.jsonl:36:{"id":"ex-euclidean-algorithm-on-consecutive-fibonacci-numbers","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"established-knowledge","urls":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Euclidean_algorithm","https://mathworld.wolfram.com/EuclideanAlgorithm.html","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fibonacci_sequence"],"rationale":"Two established results, neither located in the exact form the item states. (1) Consecutive Fibonacci numbers are coprime: standard, and a special case of gcd(F_m,F_n) = F_gcd(m,n); no clean HTTP-200 statement surfaced (ProofWiki returns 403 to the fetcher). (2) The division count: Wikipedia and MathWorld both record that consecutive Fibonacci numbers are the worst case for the Euclidean algorithm (Lame 1844), and the standard form of that analysis is that (F_{n+2}, F_{n+1}) needs exactly n divisions, which is the item claim with n = m+1; the quotient pattern (1 repeated, then a final 2) is the routine content of the same analysis. Both are recoverable restatements of established mathematics, not invented claims, so the tag is ai-altered under evidence established-knowledge and REQUIRES Alpha concurrence. The indexing convention F_0 = 0 is stated by the item and matches the Wikipedia zeroth-element convention. Proof is a locally written double induction.\n\nALPHA CONCURS (2026-08-02). Both components are standard established mathematics. (1) gcd(F_{k+1}, F_k) = 1 for all k is a textbook result and the k-th case of gcd(F_m,F_n) = F_{gcd(m,n)}. (2) The division count is the standard statement of the Euclidean algorithm's Fibonacci worst case (Lame 1844): on (F_{n+2}, F_{n+1}) with n >= 1 the algorithm performs exactly n divisions, which is the item's claim at n = m+1; the quotient pattern (1 repeated, then a terminal 2 at (F_3,F_2) = (2,1)) is the routine content of the same analysis, not an invented embellishment. Alpha verified the claim numerically at m = 0,1,2: (2,1) -> 1 division q=2; (3,2) -> 2 divisions q=1,2; (5,3) -> 3 divisions q=1,1,2. The F_0 = 0 indexing is stated by the item and matches the standard convention, and the item explicitly disclaims Lame's optimality statement. ai-altered under the established-knowledge waiver; not ai-generated, since nothing here is AI-invented.","alpha_concurred":true,"at":"2026-08-02","alpha_reviewer":"audit-alpha (claude-opus-5)"}
research/audit/wave0-real-analysis-cauchy.provenance.jsonl:15:{"id":"lem-rat-inverse-well-defined","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"established-knowledge","urls":[],"rationale":"ESCALATION-LEVEL NOTE for Alpha: well-definedness of the reciprocal [(a,b)]^-1 := [(b,a)] on Q \\ {0} is a standard step in every set-theoretic construction of Q (Landau, Tao Analysis I S4.2, Enderton Ch. 5), but no open source stating it as a named lemma surfaced. It is not an invented claim: it is the routine representative-independence obligation attached to the sourced reciprocal formula of Wikipedia Rational number, and is directly verifiable from def-rationals in one line (ab' = a'b <=> ba' = b'a by commutativity of Z). Recorded ai-altered/established-knowledge pending Alpha's independent concurrence. Local proof written here.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-02"}
