+          "source": "lem-group-inverse-laws",
+          "source_section": "Statement",
+          "quote": "1. $e^{-1} = e$;\n2. $(g^{-1})^{-1} = g$; in particular inversion $g \\mapsto g^{-1}$ is its own\n   two-sided inverse as a map $G \\to G$, hence a bijection of $G$;\n3. $(gh)^{-1} = h^{-1} g^{-1}$;\n4. $(gh)^{-1} = g^{-1} h^{-1}$ holds **if and only if** $g$ and $h$ commute. So\n   the reversal of order in claim 3 cannot be dropped in general, and in an\n   abelian group it may be.",
+          "uses": [
+            "1.1",
+            "1.2"
+          ]
+        },
+        {
+          "fact": "L3",
+          "source": "def-homeomorphism-and-open-maps",
+          "source_section": "Definition",
+          "quote": "- $f$ is an **open map** if $f[U]$ is open in $Y$ for every open $U \\subseteq X$.\n- $f$ is a **closed map** if $f[F]$ is closed in $Y$ for every closed\n  $F \\subseteq X$.\n- $f$ is a **homeomorphism** if $f$ is a continuous bijection whose inverse\n  $f^{-1} : Y \\to X$ is also continuous. The spaces are **homeomorphic**, written\n  $X \\cong Y$, when a homeomorphism $X \\to Y$ exists.\n- $f$ is an **embedding** if $f$ is injective and the corestriction\n  $f_0 : X \\to f[X]$, $f_0(x) = f(x)$, is a homeomorphism onto $f[X]$ carrying\n  the subspace topology inherited from $Y$ ([[def-subspace-topology-top]]).",
+          "uses": [
+            "2.1"
+          ]
+        }
