+{"id":"def-uniform-space-by-entourages","statement":"literature-derived","proof":"not-applicable","evidence":"exact-source","urls":["https://math.berkeley.edu/~wodzicki/H104.F13/UniformStructure-alt.pdf","https://encyclopediaofmath.org/wiki/Uniform_space"],"rationale":"Wodzicki Definition 2.1 and Encyclopedia of Mathematics U1-U3 state the same proper-filter entourage axioms, quantifiers, and directions; only notation changes.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
+{"id":"lem-symmetric-entourages-form-a-base","statement":"literature-derived","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"exact-source","urls":["https://math.berkeley.edu/~wodzicki/H104.F13/UniformStructure-alt.pdf"],"rationale":"Wodzicki states that symmetric entourages form a base. The proof is a rewritten intersection-with-inverse argument. The finite-composite strengthening proposed at A3 is also Wodzicki Exercise 23 but is absent from the current Statement.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
+{"id":"thm-uniformity-induces-a-topology","statement":"literature-derived","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"exact-source","urls":["https://math.berkeley.edu/~wodzicki/H104.F13/UniformStructure-alt.pdf"],"rationale":"Wodzicki defines the uniform topology by exactly the entourage-ball neighborhood condition and proves it is a topology; the item changes notation only.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
+{"id":"def-separated-uniform-space","statement":"literature-derived","proof":"not-applicable","evidence":"exact-source","urls":["https://math.berkeley.edu/~wodzicki/H104.F13/UniformStructure-alt.pdf","https://encyclopediaofmath.org/wiki/Uniform_space"],"rationale":"Both sources define separatedness by the intersection of all entourages being the diagonal, with the same convention and no changed hypothesis.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
+{"id":"thm-separated-uniformity-iff-induced-topology-is-hausdorff","statement":"literature-derived","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"exact-source","urls":["https://math.berkeley.edu/~wodzicki/H104.F13/UniformStructure-alt.pdf","https://encyclopediaofmath.org/wiki/Uniform_space"],"rationale":"The sources state the same biconditional for the topology induced by an entourage uniformity. The local proof expands the two directions.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
+{"id":"def-uniformly-continuous-map","statement":"literature-derived","proof":"not-applicable","evidence":"exact-source","urls":["https://math.berkeley.edu/~wodzicki/H104.F13/UniformStructure-alt.pdf","https://u.math.biu.ac.il/~megereli/TGrNotes070217.pdf"],"rationale":"Wodzicki Definition 4.6 and the BIU notes give the same entourage pullback quantifiers for uniform continuity.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
+{"id":"thm-uniformly-continuous-maps-are-continuous","statement":"literature-derived","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"exact-source","urls":["https://math.berkeley.edu/~wodzicki/H104.F13/UniformStructure-alt.pdf"],"rationale":"Wodzicki states exactly that a uniformly continuous map is continuous in the respective uniform topologies; the item supplies the standard ball proof.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
+{"id":"lem-metric-uniformity-dictionary","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://math.berkeley.edu/~wodzicki/H104.F13/UniformStructure-alt.pdf","https://u.math.biu.ac.il/~megereli/TGrNotes070217.pdf"],"rationale":"The sources give the metric entourages, induced metric topology, separation, and epsilon-delta interpretation. The item packages these clauses. Unlike the source convention, the library permits an empty metric carrier while requiring filters proper; this creates the empty-carrier defect escalated in A3.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
+{"id":"def-uniform-cover-space","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"not-applicable","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://encyclopediaofmath.org/wiki/Uniform_space","https://math.berkeley.edu/~wodzicki/H104.F13/UniformStructure-alt.pdf"],"rationale":"Encyclopedia of Mathematics gives the uniform-cover axioms and Wodzicki develops the associated cover relation. The item uses an equivalent star-refinement presentation and currently overstates equivalence on the empty carrier under the library proper-filter convention.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
+{"id":"lem-entourage-and-uniform-cover-dictionary","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://encyclopediaofmath.org/wiki/Uniform_space","https://math.berkeley.edu/~wodzicki/H104.F13/UniformStructure-alt.pdf"],"rationale":"The sources give the standard equivalence between entourage and uniform-cover formulations. The item spells out both constructions; its cover-to-entourage direction needs the nonempty-carrier qualification recorded for A3.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
+{"id":"def-gauge-of-pseudometrics","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"not-applicable","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://encyclopediaofmath.org/wiki/Uniform_space","https://www.mat.univie.ac.at/~mike/teaching/ss16/general_topology.pdf","https://u.math.biu.ac.il/~megereli/TGrNotes070217.pdf"],"rationale":"The sources define a uniformity from finite intersections of pseudometric balls. The item calls the family a gauge and bundles topology and uniform-continuity clauses; under the local proper-filter convention it also needs a nonempty carrier.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
+{"id":"lem-normal-sequences-of-entourages","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"established-knowledge","urls":["https://math.berkeley.edu/~wodzicki/H104.F13/UniformStructure-alt.pdf","https://www.mat.univie.ac.at/~mike/teaching/ss16/general_topology.pdf"],"rationale":"ESCALATED TO ALPHA. Iterated symmetric entourage roots are the standard normal-sequence construction in both sources, but the Statement makes the strictly weaker foundational hypothesis DC explicit and no located source states that same choice-calibrated proposition. It is established, not novel; the recursion proof is local.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
+{"id":"lem-pseudometric-from-a-normal-entourage-sequence","statement":"literature-derived","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"exact-source","urls":["https://math.berkeley.edu/~wodzicki/H104.F13/UniformStructure-alt.pdf","https://www.mat.univie.ac.at/~mike/teaching/ss16/general_topology.pdf"],"rationale":"Both sources state the normal-sequence metrization lemma with the same dyadic control inclusions and path-infimum pseudometric construction. The proof is a notation-adjusted expansion.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
+{"id":"thm-entourage-uniformities-are-generated-by-gauges","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"established-knowledge","urls":["https://math.berkeley.edu/~wodzicki/H104.F13/UniformStructure-alt.pdf","https://www.mat.univie.ac.at/~mike/teaching/ss16/general_topology.pdf"],"rationale":"ESCALATED TO ALPHA. The sources prove every uniformity is generated by pseudometrics but assume an ordinary classical background; the item isolates DC as sufficient. The theorem is standard, but no source with the same foundational hypothesis surfaced.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
+{"id":"thm-three-definitions-of-uniform-space-are-equivalent","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"established-knowledge","urls":["https://encyclopediaofmath.org/wiki/Uniform_space","https://math.berkeley.edu/~wodzicki/H104.F13/UniformStructure-alt.pdf","https://www.mat.univie.ac.at/~mike/teaching/ss16/general_topology.pdf"],"rationale":"ESCALATED TO ALPHA. Literature gives the entourage-cover equivalence and pseudometric generation, but not this exact compound ZF/DC calibration. This is a standard synthesis, not an invented theorem; the proof composes the preceding results.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
+{"id":"def-cauchy-filter-in-a-uniform-space","statement":"literature-derived","proof":"not-applicable","evidence":"exact-source","urls":["https://math.berkeley.edu/~wodzicki/H104.F13/UniformStructure-alt.pdf","https://encyclopediaofmath.org/wiki/Uniform_space"],"rationale":"The sources use the same requirement that for every entourage a filter member have square contained in it; notation and equivalent cover wording are the only differences.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
+{"id":"lem-convergent-filters-are-cauchy","statement":"literature-derived","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"exact-source","urls":["https://math.berkeley.edu/~wodzicki/H104.F13/UniformStructure-alt.pdf"],"rationale":"Wodzicki Exercise 41 states exactly that every filter convergent in the uniform topology is Cauchy. The item supplies the standard symmetric-root proof, with a missing direct citation proposed at A3.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
+{"id":"lem-cauchy-filter-with-a-cluster-point-converges","statement":"literature-derived","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"exact-source","urls":["https://math.berkeley.edu/~wodzicki/H104.F13/UniformStructure-alt.pdf"],"rationale":"Wodzicki Exercise 45 states that every adherence point of a Cauchy filter is its convergence point. The proof expands this and needs the induced-topology citation recorded at A3.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
+{"id":"def-complete-uniform-space","statement":"literature-derived","proof":"not-applicable","evidence":"exact-source","urls":["https://math.berkeley.edu/~wodzicki/H104.F13/UniformStructure-alt.pdf","https://encyclopediaofmath.org/wiki/Complete_uniform_space"],"rationale":"Both sources define completeness as convergence of every Cauchy filter, exactly as the item does.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
+{"id":"def-uniform-embedding-and-uniform-isomorphism","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"not-applicable","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://encyclopediaofmath.org/wiki/Uniform_space","https://math.berkeley.edu/~wodzicki/H104.F13/UniformStructure-alt.pdf"],"rationale":"The literature uses the same uniform-isomorphism and subspace-uniformity notions. The item packages the embedding and isomorphism definitions in library notation.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
+{"id":"def-completion-of-a-uniform-space","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"not-applicable","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://encyclopediaofmath.org/wiki/Uniform_space","https://math.berkeley.edu/~wodzicki/H104.F13/UniformStructure-alt.pdf","https://encyclopediaofmath.org/wiki/Complete_uniform_space"],"rationale":"The sources define completion by a complete separated target with dense canonical image and induced original uniformity. The item explicitly permits a noninjective Hausdorff reflection map, the equivalent convention for nonseparated inputs.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
+{"id":"lem-minimal-cauchy-filter-associated-to-a-cauchy-filter","statement":"literature-derived","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"exact-source","urls":["https://math.berkeley.edu/~wodzicki/H104.F13/UniformStructure-alt.pdf","https://www.boletin.math.org.mx/pdf/2/34/BSMM%282%29.34.11-21.pdf"],"rationale":"Wodzicki 6.1.2-6.1.5 and Hunt give the canonical unique minimal Cauchy filter contained in a Cauchy filter. A3 proposes stating the sourced principal-filter clause needed downstream.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
+{"id":"lem-entourages-on-minimal-cauchy-filters-form-a-separated-uniformity","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://math.berkeley.edu/~wodzicki/H104.F13/UniformStructure-alt.pdf","https://www.boletin.math.org.mx/pdf/2/34/BSMM%282%29.34.11-21.pdf"],"rationale":"Wodzicki restricts the standard filter-base uniformity to minimal Cauchy filters and states it is Hausdorff. The item spells out the relation. Its proof omits the nonempty-carrier discharge supplied by the sourced principal-filter clause.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
+{"id":"lem-minimal-cauchy-filter-space-is-complete","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://math.berkeley.edu/~wodzicki/H104.F13/UniformStructure-alt.pdf","https://www.boletin.math.org.mx/pdf/2/34/BSMM%282%29.34.11-21.pdf"],"rationale":"The sources construct the completion from minimal Cauchy filters; completeness of that space is part of the construction. The item isolates and expands that component.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
+{"id":"lem-point-filters-are-dense-in-the-minimal-cauchy-filter-space","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://math.berkeley.edu/~wodzicki/H104.F13/UniformStructure-alt.pdf","https://www.boletin.math.org.mx/pdf/2/34/BSMM%282%29.34.11-21.pdf"],"rationale":"Wodzicki 6.1.6-6.2.4 gives the point-neighborhood-filter map, uniform continuity, and density. The item packages these conclusions; A3 proposes the member-contains-the-point clause actually used later.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
+{"id":"thm-hausdorff-completion-of-a-uniform-space","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://math.berkeley.edu/~wodzicki/H104.F13/UniformStructure-alt.pdf","https://encyclopediaofmath.org/wiki/Uniform_space","https://encyclopediaofmath.org/wiki/Complete_uniform_space"],"rationale":"The sources state existence of a dense complete Hausdorff completion; Wodzicki also identifies when the canonical map reflects the original uniformity. The item packages the reflection and embedding criterion.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
+{"id":"thm-uniform-completion-universal-property-and-uniqueness","statement":"literature-derived","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"exact-source","urls":["https://math.berkeley.edu/~wodzicki/H104.F13/UniformStructure-alt.pdf","https://encyclopediaofmath.org/wiki/Uniform_space"],"rationale":"Wodzicki 6.2.3 states the same unique extension of every uniformly continuous map into a complete Hausdorff uniform space. Uniqueness of completions is its exact categorical consequence.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
