items/cex-subset-closed-under-addition-but-not-scaling.md:91:4.1 So $Q$ contains the zero vector and is closed under addition, by steps 1.2 and 2.1, and is not a linear subspace, by step 3.1; the claim of [L8] is therefore false. The failure can also be read in the additive group: $(-1)e_0 = -e_0$ lies outside $Q$, so $Q$ is not even a subgroup of the additive group of $\mathbb{R}^{2}$, whereas a linear subspace always is. [step 1.2, step 2.1, step 3.1, L5, L6, L8] ∎
