+          "source_section": "Statement",
+          "quote": "The topology of a completely regular space is induced by the gauge of pseudometrics $p_f(x,y)=|f(x)-f(y)|$, where $f:X\\to[0,1]$ ranges over continuous maps.",
+          "uses": [
+            "1.2"
+          ]
+        },
+        {
+          "fact": "L4",
+          "source": "def-gauge-of-pseudometrics",
+          "source_section": "Definition",
+          "quote": "For an already given uniformity $\\mathcal U$ on $X$, a pseudometric $p$ is\n**uniformly continuous for $\\mathcal U$** when\n$$\n\\{(x,y):p(x,y)<\\varepsilon\\}\\in\\mathcal U\n$$\nfor every $\\varepsilon>0$. With this terminology, each member of a gauge is\nuniformly continuous for the uniformity generated by that gauge.",
+          "uses": [
+            "1.2"
+          ]
+        }
+      ],
+      "derivations": [
+        {
+          "id": "step-1.1",
+          "claim": "A separated-uniformizable space is completely regular by [L1] and Hausdorff by [L2], hence $T_1$ and therefore Tychonoff by [L3]. [L1, L2, L3]",
